🚽 Test case: issue #313 -- a `cap`-returning call used where an integer
🚽 is wanted, which above all means an `edgy`/`goon` CONDITION.
🚽
🚽 unbox_native_numeric_result() (ast.c) knew every scalar type except
🚽 VAR_BOOL, so `edgy (yes())` hit its `default: return false` and the
🚽 caller reported "cannot be used in an integer context".
🚽
🚽 ── Why this was worse than an error ──────────────────────────────────
🚽 It FAILED OPEN. The condition evaluated to 0, so `edgy (yes())` took
🚽 the false branch and a program ignoring stderr quietly did the wrong
🚽 thing -- the same failure mode as #296 and #303. That is why the
🚽 `edgy (yes())` line below asserts the TRUE branch ran: an
🚽 implementation that reported the error and evaluated 0 would print
🚽 nothing there and fail this fixture, rather than merely printing an
🚽 extra diagnostic.
🚽
🚽 ── What is deliberately NOT here ─────────────────────────────────────
🚽 `rizz n = yes();` is rejected -- "expected int, got bool" -- and that
🚽 is NOT this bug: `rizz n = b;` for a plain `cap b` is rejected
🚽 identically, with no call involved. It is a declaration type rule,
🚽 consistent either way, so pinning it here would mislabel it as part of
🚽 this fix.
cap yes() {
    bussin W;
}

cap no() {
    bussin L;
}

skibidi main {
    🚽 the shape the issue is about: a call directly as a condition
    edgy (yes()) {
        yapping("edgy true");
    }

    🚽 ... and the false case, so a fix that hardcoded "true" would fail
    edgy (no()) {
        yapping("NOT REACHED");
    } amogus {
        yapping("edgy false");
    }

    🚽 a loop condition, and mixed into a larger boolean expression
    rizz n = 0;
    goon (n < 2 && yes()) {
        n = n + 1;
    }
    yapping("goon ran %d", n);

    🚽 an integer context that is not a condition
    yapping("negated %d", !yes());
    bussin 0;
}
